# NOVELTY AUDIT (round 2, prior-art role) — 2026-09-17

Scope: novelty / prior-art claims only. Round-1 work (number reproduction,
BM25/ladder/PerLTQA checks, LoCoMo gold dispute, portability) is not repeated.
READ-ONLY on all source trees; writes only in this directory. No internet
access was available: every novelty judgement below carries that limitation,
and no paper was fetched, quoted, or numerically cited from memory.

## 1. VERDICT (3 lines)

1. No CRITICAL finding: every published number rechecked here reproduces
   (ITQ −2.27/−0.13; k384 sym 54.18 vs float 43.69; asym 60.79→61.09), and
   no fabricated citation was found in the lit_r2 maps.
2. The flagship "ITQ worse than random" is NOT reviewer-ready as a general
   claim: one ITQ init vs 3 production (+7 audit) random seeds, 50 fixed
   iters with only ~2% loss drop and no convergence trace, per-archive fits
   at n/k 3–16; RealTalk/qscale direction is solid (0/10 rotations), the
   PerLTQA half is null.
3. Every novelty claim must stay hedged (PLAUSIBLY NEW instance at best, else
   CANNOT JUDGE without literature access); Sec. 5 gives safe wording per claim.

## 2. Findings (short table; detail in Sec. 3)

| ID | Sev | Claim audited | Check performed | Result |
|----|-----|---------------|-----------------|--------|
| F1 | HIGH | ITQ setup is reviewer-fair | Read impl, seeds, loss diags | Not fair: 1 init vs 3+7 |
| N1 | HIGH | ITQ<random is new-to-field | Training recall, no web | Plausibly new instance only |
| N2 | MED | Size-damage r=-0.78 is new | Recall + round-1 limits | Plausibly new, n=10, Hit@10-only |
| N3 | MED | Balanced-but-worse is new | Recall + MED confound | Plausibly new instance, no causality |
| N4 | MED | Sigma-collapse mechanism new | Textbook reasoning check | Principle likely known; split is new |
| N5 | HIGH | sign() adds ~10pp, general | Traced 54.18/43.69 source | k384 RealTalk only; k96 contradicts |
| N6 | HIGH | Few-hundred regime unstudied | Internal scan only | Cannot judge; null is not evidence |
| N7 | LOW | Fair-baseline method is new | Recall of field practice | Likely known hygiene; honest use is real |
| N8 | LOW | No arm wins both is a finding | Read REPORT Sec. 6 | Pipeline regularity, not a law |
| C1 | LOW | lit_r2 cites fake papers | Scanned all cited names | No fabrication; 3 vague refs flagged |

Limitation (applies to N1–N8, C1): NO internet. Each "likely known /
plausibly new" below is training-recall only, stated without invented
titles, authors, or years.

## 3. Per-finding detail

### F1. ITQ was given one draw against its rival's distribution (HIGH)

- Impl: `top10_comparison_r1/math_r1/quant/quant_math.py`
  lines 63/68-69 (`RAND_SEEDS`=3 seeds, `ITQ_ITERS`=50, `ITQ_SEED`=single),
  lines 140-158 (`fit_itq`: init once via `random_orthogonal`, alternate
  B/R 50x, store only init/final loss, no trace, no stopping rule),
  line 492 (single `fit_itq(C)` call per archive; RAND loop varies 3 seeds).
- Report: `math_r1/quant/REPORT.md` lines 35-42 (50 iters, init seed
  20260916; `RAND_20260916` = same matrix ITQ starts from — paired start).
- Production contrast is therefore 1 ITQ solution vs 3 RAND draws per
  archive. Round-1 claims audit extended RAND to 7 fresh seeds but kept
  exactly 1 ITQ solution (`audit_hard_r1/CONSOLIDATED_AUDIT.md` review
  section: "does NOT vary seven ITQ initializations").
- Convergence: from `math_r1/quant/RESULTS.json` `itq_diagnostics`,
  relative loss improvement is 1.6–2.1% (RealTalk) and 2.2–2.4%
  (PerLTQA) on all 40 archives — the optimizer moves ~2% from a random
  start and stops at a fixed 50 with no per-iter curve. A reviewer cannot
  tell "converged" from "stuck near init".
- Sample regime: per-archive N is 410–1548 (RealTalk) and 293–546
  (PerLTQA) for a 96x96 rotation (4560 free parameters), i.e. n/k of
  ~3–16. Scalar count n*k exceeds 4560, so this is not naive
  overfitting, but estimating a 96-dim rotation from ~400 points with
  no held-out check and no multi-restart is below the bar for a
  "method M fails" claim. ITQ is non-convex alternating minimization:
  one init = one local optimum.
- Commands run (read-only): grep for seed/iter lines above; python3 read
  of `RESULTS.json` summaries (ITQ/qscale 32.77 vs RANDs
  35.18/35.32/34.61 RealTalk; 78.91 vs 78.77/79.48/78.86 PerLTQA),
  per-seed ITQ−RAND Hit@10 (−2.41/−2.55/−1.84 RealTalk, all CIs exclude
  0; +0.15/−0.57/+0.05 PerLTQA, all ns), per-archive loss ratios.
- New beyond round 1: round 1 showed RAND-side robustness (7 fresh
  seeds); the gap I add is the ITQ side was never varied, convergence
  was never shown, and the paired-start strength (ITQ worsened the
  exact matrix it started from, −2.41 vs RAND_20260916) is the only
  piece that survives strict review — and it is n=1 init.

### N1. "ITQ worse than random — not in the literature" (HIGH, hedged)

- Claims: `REPORT.md` lines 109-112 (−2.27/−0.13, "any rotation does
  this damage"); `REPORT.md` lines 139-144 (scope: dense image
  descriptors + Euclidean neighbours vs TF-IDF/SVD + gold retrieval);
  `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 44-46 (claim 1 of "genuinely ours").
- Numbers reproduce (see F1 command results; mean of the three
  per-seed diffs = −2.27 RealTalk, −0.12 PerLTQA). Not a number dispute.
- Training recall (UNVERIFIED, no web): I recall ITQ evaluated on dense
  visual-descriptor ANN benchmarks, and I recall no evaluation of ITQ
  on sparse TF-IDF/SVD sign codes, per-archive small-n fits, or gold
  retrieval metrics. Negative recall is weak evidence by nature.
- Judgement: PLAUSIBLY NEW as a measured instance on this pipeline;
  CANNOT be upgraded to "new-to-field" without the fetch queue
  (quant `FETCH_QUEUE.md` ranks 1/5/6/7) returning a documented null.
  The explanatory principle (reconstruction-optimal ≠
  retrieval-optimal, cf. score-aware quantization precedent in round-1
  lit map) is LIKELY KNOWN by analogy and must be cited as analogy,
  not coverage.
- Safe wording: "On per-archive TF-IDF/SVD sign codes at 12 B/doc, one
  ITQ solution (50 iters, single init) did not beat any of 3 (resp. 10
  incl. audit) random rotations on RealTalk/qscale (−2.27 pp); PerLTQA
  was null (−0.13 pp, inside seed noise). Whether this generalizes
  beyond this pipeline is untested."

### N2. "Rotation damage scales with archive size, r=−0.78" (MED)

- Claims: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 47-49; `REPORT.md` lines 122-130
  (−6.56 vs −23.13 pp, PerLTQA gap "explained by size alone").
- Training recall: I recall no reported "rotation damage grows with n
  at fixed k" curve for sign codes. PLAUSIBLY NEW as a regularity;
  CANNOT JUDGE field-novelty without the hubness/concentration +
  small-sample fetches (quant `FETCH_QUEUE.md` ranks 4/5).
- Internal limits (partly round-1, preserved not repeated: n=10
  archives; association weakens to −0.56/−0.29 on FR@3; one archive
  RT04 is helped by rotation yet r barely moves): my addition is that
  "alone explains the cross-benchmark difference" is between-benchmark
  accounting, not a within-mechanism causal finding — n may proxy
  content (large archives = chattiest/multi-topic). The cheap
  discriminator (partial correlations of spectral-Gini / hubness skew
  vs damage conditioning on n; size-matched subsample refit) was never
  run.
- Safe wording: "Exploratory (n=10, Hit@10): damage correlates with
  archive size; mechanism open (spectral concentration vs hubness vs
  content confound)."

### N3. "Bit balance anti-correlates with retrieval" (MED)

- Claims: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 50-52 (ranges 0.309–0.658 → 0.459–
  0.545); `REPORT.md` lines 132-134; quant `REPORT.md` bit-balance
  paragraph (FULL mean ~0.492, rotated ~0.500).
- Training recall: balance/maximum-entropy as a design goal for
  spectral hashing / ITQ-style methods is LIKELY KNOWN folklore; I
  recall no "balanced-but-worse at fixed bits with gold retrieval"
  report. PLAUSIBLY NEW as a measured instance; causality NOT
  established — rotation changes far more than balance, and the MED
  arm (balance 0.500 by construction, neutral/tiny-hurt) changes query
  thresholds too, so it is not a pure balance intervention (as the
  round-1 consolidated review also notes).
- Safe wording: "Rotation tightened bit balance and retrieval fell;
  a co-occurrence, not a causal finding. Do not claim balance hurts."

### N4. "Sigma-division collapses at high k while asym rises" (MED)

- Claims: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 53-54; `DECISION_TESTS.md` T3 section
  (k<n enforced, 0 constant dims, qscale −3.60 / sym −5.17 persist,
  asym +0.30; lines for 60.79→61.09 verified in
  `coordinator/T3_PERLTQA_KLTN.json` lines 79/94).
- The scorer-split observation (standardized arms fall, unstandardized
  rise on identical subspaces) is the durable, plausibly new-to-this-
  pipeline part. The mechanism sentence ("dividing by the tiny σ of
  genuine near-singular trailing directions") is standard whitening
  reasoning — amplifying low-variance coordinates by 1/σ is textbook —
  so LIKELY KNOWN as a principle. Per the already-known-issues list,
  the sigma explanation is incomplete/partly wrong; I do not re-litigate
  it, only its novelty: do not present the mechanism as a discovery.
- Safe wording: "At high k the standardized scorers decline where the
  unstandardized readouts keep rising (PerLTQA T3); consistent with
  σ-whitening of tail directions, mechanism not isolated."

### N5. "sign() adds ~10pp over its float source" (HIGH — scope)

- Claim: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 57-58 (k=384: sym 54.18 vs float 43.69).
- Provenance traced: `coordinator/ladder2.log` lines 21/23 (RealTalk
  k384/sym Hit@10 54.18, k384/float 43.69). So the comparison is
  RealTalk-only, k=384-only, and the float is the ladder's cosine float
  arm — while at k=96 the sign LOSES to the uncompressed float
  (`REPORT.md` lines 24-26: float_std 48.51 vs sign96 46.68).
- Judgement: PLAUSIBLY NEW as a high-k instance (sign equalizing per-dim
  votes can beat raw cosine when tail variance is misleading), but the
  "adds" framing inverts with k and is scorer/benchmark-specific.
  Stated generally it is false on the project's own k=96 table.
- Safe wording: "On RealTalk at k=384 only, the sign code beat the
  ladder float arm (54.18 vs 43.69); at k=96 the float wins. Do not
  quote without k, benchmark, and scorer."

### N6. "Few-hundred-doc regime / ≤16B gold Hit@10 unstudied" (HIGH)

- Claims: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 111-113 ("appears unstudied"); `REPORT.md`
  lines 282-283 ("Literature search found no source...").
- What was actually searched: internal inventory
  (`DO_NOT_RESCAN.md`, `GAP_TO_100.md`, round-1 `LITERATURE_MAP.md`) —
  a local scan, not the field. An internal null ("we found none in our
  scanned set") cannot support a field claim ("unstudied") without the
  web fetches in the three `FETCH_QUEUE.md` files, none executed.
- Training recall (weak): BEIR-era corpora I recall are ~10^3–10^6
  (smallest recalled ~3–5K), an order above our 293–1548/archive; that
  makes a gap plausible but proves nothing. CANNOT JUDGE WITHOUT
  LITERATURE ACCESS.
- Safe wording: "We found no source in our scanned set reporting gold
  Hit@10@K=10 from ≤12–16 B/doc codes on few-hundred-document
  per-archive collections; we did not survey the field."

### N7. "Fair-baseline methodology" as novelty (LOW)

- Claim: `FINAL_STATE.md` lines 55-56 ("give the competitor your own
  tokenizer before claiming a win").
- Judgement: LIKELY KNOWN as practice (tuned-baseline / ablation-hygiene
  norms; BEIR-era "BM25 is a strong baseline" discussion; ANN framing of
  first stage as recall/cost device). The real contribution is the
  honest application (the +10.35 RealTalk correction reversing the
  headline), not the method. Publish as conduct, not as invention.
- Note: the +10.35 correction is RealTalk-only (already-known issue);
  it must not travel as a cross-benchmark constant.

### N8. "No arm has ever won on both benchmarks" (LOW)

- Claim: `REPORT.md` lines 272-275.
- Judgement: an honest pipeline-level regularity (channel ablation, IDF
  power, component shift all reverse signs), PLAUSIBLY NEW as an
  observation about this system. It is not a general law (2 benchmarks,
  wide intervals on 10 RealTalk archives, exploratory) and reversals
  across collections are common in IR. Keep as a caution, not a finding.

### C1. lit_r2 citation hygiene (LOW — passes, 3 flags)

